title ML-KEM sealed session GREEN — the post-quantum confidential door, sibling of sealed_session.rye
prompt Continue the loop Lindy-first crux-first; approve my own design rounds and make progress before checking in; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates; a breach rename records a cairn first.
think With the post-quantum SIGNING identity landed (mldsa_identity.rye), the next durable crux from that round's recommend is the confidentiality counterpart: the post-quantum SEALED SESSION over ML-KEM, the sibling of crypto/sealed_session.rye and kumara_sealed.rye. The pivot doc names it directly — ML-KEM is the successor to X25519, and the sealed-session sibling of kumara_sealed.rye becomes buildable once encaps/decaps are GREEN, which they are. Identity + confidentiality are the two front doors that give the whole post-quantum arc its reason for being; this closes the second.
think Read crypto/sealed_session.rye end to end for the frame shape and the ten-part round-trip/tamper proof, then the ML-KEM encaps/decaps/keygen signatures and their Zig oracle calls (Kem.SecretKey.fromBytes(dk).decaps(c), Kem.PublicKey.fromBytes(ek).encapsDeterministic(m)). The KEM composition differs from the DH box: freshness lives inside encaps (a fresh 32-byte message m), not in an ephemeral keypair. ML-KEM's shared secret K is already bound to ek and the ciphertext via the FO transform's G(m || H(ek)), so a fresh m yields a fresh K, and a wrong recipient's decaps yields the implicit-rejection secret rather than failing — the AEAD tag is what rejects.
think Authored crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye comptime-parameterized per set (Params {k, eta1, du, dv}; mlkem_512/768/1024; default mlkem_768, NIST category 3). The recipient holds a long-term (ek, dk) keypair; seal encapsulates to ek under a fresh m, derives session key+nonce from BLAKE2b-512(K || H(ek)) — so nonce is fresh per message — and seals with ChaCha20-Poly1305 binding H(ek) as aad; open decapsulates the KEM ciphertext back to K and returns plaintext only when the tag verifies. Frame: magic GPQ1 || kem-ciphertext(fixed per set) || cipher-len(u64 BE) || ciphertext || tag. Mirrored the classical module's helpers and TAME discipline (opening triad, >=2 asserts per fn, bounded loops, named errors).
think Proof at all three sets: round-trip across seven plaintext lengths (empty..64 KiB), confidentiality (ciphertext != plaintext), sealed-box freshness (two m's -> different frames both opening), determinism (fixed m -> byte-identical frames), and five refusals — wrong recipient, tampered KEM ciphertext, tampered ciphertext body, tampered tag, plus bad magic and two truncations. The DOUBLED ORACLE ties it to the standard: Zig std.crypto SecretKey.decaps of the very KEM ciphertext our seal embeds recovers the same shared secret our seal used, so the frame interoperates with NIST FIPS 203. Built clean on the first try; GREEN on the first real run at all three sets.
obs GREEN — crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye, the post-quantum sealed session, proven end to end at ML-KEM-512/768/1024, the frame interoperating with Zig's independent std.crypto ml_kem. Count guard moved 73->74 files (fifty primitives + twenty-four compositions); bijection intact; TAME bans clean; width-check exit 0. Docs synced same round: crypto/README.md and docs/CRYPTO.md carry the mlkem_sealed row and 73->74 / 23->24 counts; the suite witness registers it and updated all four spelled counts.
obs With this and mldsa_identity landed together, both post-quantum front doors now stand: a keeper's name can be SIGNED with ML-DSA and a message SEALED to that keeper with ML-KEM, each proven twice over and holding no real key. The two are siblings of kumara_identity.rye and sealed_session.rye respectively — the classical arc's post-quantum mirror is complete at the composition layer.
